<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's Sunny! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Fill in a form with their name and age with adult help. Interactive: Write simple phrases with errors on a drawing or postcard.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write their name and simple phrases on a weather-related drawing (e.g., labeling a personal drawing of a sunny day with their name and a simple phrase describing the drawing). Can write simple phrases related to the weather (e.g., "It is sunny.") with support. Can label pictures of different weather conditions (e.g., drawing a cloud and writing "cloudy").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and displays a large, colorful drawing of a sunny day. The teacher asks, "What is the weather today?" and encourages students to chorally respond, "It is sunny!" The teacher then writes "It is sunny!" on the board next to the drawing, pointing to each word as they say it. Students then receive a small paper with a sun outline to draw inside. Teacher says, "Draw the sun! And write 'sun' next to it, like this." The teacher models writing 'sun' on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the word "sunny" written clearly. The teacher slowly pronounces "/s/unny," emphasizing the initial /s/ sound, and traces the letters in the air. The teacher then writes "It is sunny." on the board with a marker, modeling proper letter formation for first graders, from left to right. The teacher also shows a picture of rain and writes "It is raining." emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher models how to write their name on a simple line.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a cloudy day?" (pointing to the "It is sunny!" board drawing) (Expected answer: "No, sunny!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound does 'sunny' start with?" (Expected answer: "/s/")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated weather chart with simple sentences, large flashcards of weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) with the corresponding word/phrase written below each image. Genre: Picture labels and simple descriptive sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Talking about weather: Describe simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large weather chart with pictures of "sunny," "rainy," "cloudy," and "windy" days, each with its label (e.g., "It is sunny."). The teacher points to each picture and reads the sentence, asking students to repeat. "What is this? It is sunny. Repeat!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are given small individual flashcards with weather pictures (without text). The teacher calls out a weather condition, "It is cloudy!" and students hold up the corresponding picture. The teacher then writes "It is cloudy." on the board and asks students to point to the word "cloudy".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False activity: The teacher holds up a picture of a rainy day but says, "It is sunny." Students respond "False!" or "No!" The teacher then says, "It is raining." and students respond "True!" or "Yes!".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple phrases (Subject + verb + adjective: "It is sunny.", "It is rainy.", "It is cloudy."). Vocabulary: sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, name, age.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Tracing Weather Phrases.</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different weather conditions (sun, rain, cloud, wind). Below each picture, there is a dotted sentence: "It is sunny.", "It is rainy.", "It is cloudy.", "It is windy." Students use pencils to trace the dotted letters to form the complete sentences. The teacher circulates, providing individual help and correcting pencil grip/letter formation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Complete the Weather Sentence.</b> Students get another worksheet with weather pictures. Below each picture, there is an incomplete sentence: "It is ______." The teacher provides a word bank on the board: "sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy". Students look at the picture and write the correct weather word to complete the sentence. The teacher guides them to sound out words, focusing on initial sounds like /w/ in "windy" or /r/ in "rainy."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Writing & Peer Check: My Name and Weather.</b> Students receive a small card (like a mini postcard) with a blank space for a drawing and two lines: "My name is: _____" and "The weather is: _____." They draw a picture of their favorite weather (e.g., a sunny day). Then, with adult help (teacher or aide walking around), they fill in their name and choose a weather word from the board to complete the second sentence. Students then turn to a partner, show their card, and the partner checks if the name and weather word are there, giving a thumbs up.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – "My Sunny Day Drawing & Description".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are given a large piece of paper and crayons. The teacher instructs them, "Today, you will draw a beautiful sunny day! Draw the sun, trees, flowers, whatever you like for a sunny day." After drawing, the teacher says, "Now, write your name on the paper, and write 'It is sunny!' on your drawing. If you want, you can also write another word like 'sun' or 'flowers'." The teacher provides a written model of "It is sunny!" and "My name is: ______" on the board for reference. Students work individually, focusing on creating their drawing and writing their name and the simple phrase. Teacher circulates, offering support with spelling and letter formation as needed, ensuring they attempt to write the target phrase. Students are encouraged to sound out the initial letter /s/ in "sunny."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Collection of Student Drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Sunny Day Drawing & Description" (from Stage 4).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of writing:</b> Student attempts to write their name and the phrase "It is sunny!" (legibility appropriate for 1st grade).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Includes "sunny" and their name. (e.g., "It is sunny.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effort:</b> Shows engagement in attempting the writing task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up their drawing. The teacher asks:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write my name on my drawing?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write 'It is sunny!'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students to think about their writing today:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today's writing focus on "It is sunny!" went well, with most students attempting to write the phrase and their names. The tracing and fill-in-the-blank activities were effective for building confidence in letter formation and word recognition. Some students still need significant help with fine motor skills for writing, so more individual guidance and pre-writing activities (like drawing lines and circles) would be beneficial. The phonemic awareness for /s/ and /r/ was integrated, but more consistent practice with letter sounds linked to written words is needed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> For Lesson 5, students will integrate their listening, reading, speaking, and writing skills to create a class weather report or a small "Weather Book" using the learned vocabulary and phrases. This will involve summarizing information and applying all skills in a practical project, preparing for a more comprehensive review of weather conditions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">2-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>